body:
@allenwp 
Hello, allenwp

I still believe this is an engine issue. The `self_modulate` you mentioned works correctly in my tests to compensate for the problem.

After further investigation, I think the root cause lies in [blit.glsl](https://github.com/godotengine/godot/blob/master/servers/rendering/renderer_rd/shaders/blit.glsl):

```glsl
if (data.target_color_space == COLOR_SPACE_REC709_LINEAR) {
    if (data.source_is_srgb == true) {
        color.rgb = srgb_to_linear(color.rgb);
    }

    color.rgb = clamp(color.rgb, vec3(0.0), vec3(data.output_max_value));
    color.rgb *= data.reference_multiplier;
} else if (data.target_color_space == COLOR_SPACE_REC709_NONLINEAR_SRGB)
```

In this shader, whenever the target color space is linear (`COLOR_SPACE_REC709_LINEAR`), the color is multiplied by `reference_multiplier`. This multiplier is intended for adjusting SDR content brightness. However, if the source image itself is already HDR content in linear space, it should not be subjected to this luminance adjustment via `reference_multiplier`. This behavior directly corresponds to the issue I'm encountering.

Further testing revealed that dividing by 2 exactly compensated because my Windows system's SDR reference luminance was close to `reference_multiplier = 2`. Changing this value introduces deviations.

My new workaround code looks like this:

```csharp
var multiplier = 80 / DisplayServer.WindowGetHdrOutputCurrentReferenceLuminance();
var color = Colors.White.SrgbToLinear();
color *= multiplier;
color.A = 1;
color = color.LinearToSrgb();
SelfModulate = color;
```

Since you mentioned you couldn't reproduce the issue using my MRP, I've attached several images below:

- Figure 1: Unmodified capture via WGC (Windows Graphics Capture) in HDR  

<img width="2560" height="1440" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/96815288-34ff-4e32-a466-8729866c8867" />

- Figure 1.1: Unmodified screen photo taken with a smartphone  

<img width="4000" height="1824" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/2b0aa0ba-ca74-4e76-abfc-747d458f4ed8" />

- Figure 2: Manually corrected capture via WGC in HDR 

<img width="2560" height="1440" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/32d0ede7-dc40-467c-9911-34e71626ea0c" />

- Figure 2.2: Manually corrected screen photo taken with a smartphone  

<img width="4000" height="1824" alt="Image" src="https://github.com/user-attachments/assets/f0b52974-4644-481f-8058-f164301be8a3" />

If you also agree that this stems from an engine-level issue, please consider reopening the related GitHub Issue. Otherwise, if this behavior is intentional design, then this thread can serve as a documented workaround solution.

I forgot to mention something to you. The screenshots I added show the process of capturing directly through WGC, sharing the captured frame via `ID3D12Resource`, and copying it to a `RenderDevice` texture. No CPU processing or file I/O is involved at any point, and the entire process stays in scRGB.

So this isn't an EXR-specific issue. The same problem occurs with textures in linear color space in general. This results in HDR textures having their brightness adjusted twice, effectively applying a double brightness adjustment.

I can walk you through the pipeline to help you verify it. This is just a temporary test implementation, with plenty of hardcoded values and potential errors, but it should be sufficient for understanding the overall process.

<details> 

<summary>C# code</summary>

```C#
using System.Runtime.InteropServices;
using Godot;
using Windows.Graphics.Capture;
using Windows.Win32;
using Windows.Win32.Foundation;
using Windows.Win32.Graphics.Direct3D11;
using Windows.Win32.Graphics.Direct3D12;
using Windows.Win32.Graphics.Dxgi;
using Windows.Win32.Graphics.Gdi;
using Windows.Win32.System.WinRT.Graphics.Capture;
using Timer = Godot.Timer;

namespace HdrCapture;

public struct SharedTexture
{
    public ID3D11Texture2D Texture2D;
    public SafeHandle TextureHandle;

    public ID3D11Fence Fence;
    public SafeHandle FenceHandle;

    public ulong FenceValue;
}

public partial class Capture : TextureRect
{
    private Rid _rid;

    [Export]
    public TextureRect Control { get; set; }

    // Called when the node enters the scene tree for the first time.
    public override void _Ready()
    {
        DisplayServer.WindowSetMode(DisplayServer.WindowMode.Minimized);
        var t = new Timer { OneShot = true, WaitTime = 0.5 };
        t.Timeout += CaptureFrame;
        AddChild(t);
        t.Start();
        var multiplier = 80 / DisplayServer.WindowGetHdrOutputCurrentReferenceLuminance();
        var color = Colors.White.SrgbToLinear();
        color *= multiplier;
        color.A = 1;
        color = color.LinearToSrgb();
        SelfModulate = color;
    }

    private static SharedTexture CreateSharedTexture(ID3D11Texture2D source)
    {
        source.GetDesc(out var desc);
        desc.MiscFlags |= D3D11_RESOURCE_MISC_FLAG.D3D11_RESOURCE_MISC_SHARED_NTHANDLE;
        var device = DirectManager.Instance.D3D11Device;
        device.CreateTexture2D(pDesc: desc, ppTexture2D: out var shared);
        var resource = (IDXGIResource1)shared;
        resource.CreateSharedHandle(
            dwAccess: (uint)GENERIC_ACCESS_RIGHTS.GENERIC_ALL,
            pHandle: out var textureHandle
        );
        ((ID3D11Device5)device).CreateFence(
            0,
            D3D11_FENCE_FLAG.D3D11_FENCE_FLAG_SHARED,
            out ID3D11Fence fence
        );
        fence.CreateSharedHandle(
            dwAccess: (uint)GENERIC_ACCESS_RIGHTS.GENERIC_ALL,
            pHandle: out var fenceHandle
        );
        var context = (ID3D11DeviceContext4)DirectManager.Instance.D3D11Context;
        context.CopyResource(shared, source);
        context.Signal(fence, 0);
        context.Flush();
        return new SharedTexture
        {
            Texture2D = shared,
            TextureHandle = textureHandle,
            Fence = fence,
            FenceHandle = fenceHandle,
            FenceValue = 0,
        };
    }

    private void CaptureFrame()
    {
        var wgcCapture = new WgcCapture(DirectManager.Instance.D3D11Device);
        PInvoke.GetCursorPos(out var point);
        var monitor = PInvoke.MonitorFromPoint(point, MONITOR_FROM_FLAGS.MONITOR_DEFAULTTONEAREST);
        GraphicsCaptureItem
            .As<IGraphicsCaptureItemInterop>()
            .CreateForMonitor(
                monitor,
                new Guid("79C3F95B-31F7-4EC2-A464-632EF5D30760"),
                out var pUkn
            );
        var target = GraphicsCaptureItem.FromAbi(pUkn);
        Marshal.Release(pUkn);
        var texture = wgcCapture.Capture(target);
        var shared = CreateSharedTexture(texture);

        var rd = RenderingServer.GetRenderingDevice();
        var d3d12Device = (ID3D12Device)
            Marshal.GetObjectForIUnknown(
                (nint)
                    rd.GetDriverResource(RenderingDevice.DriverResource.LogicalDevice, new Rid(), 0)
            );
        var commandQueue = (ID3D12CommandQueue)
            Marshal.GetObjectForIUnknown(
                (nint)
                    rd.GetDriverResource(RenderingDevice.DriverResource.CommandQueue, new Rid(), 0)
            );
        d3d12Device.OpenSharedHandle(shared.TextureHandle, out ID3D12Resource resource);
        d3d12Device.OpenSharedHandle(shared.FenceHandle, out ID3D12Fence fence);
        commandQueue.Wait(fence, shared.FenceValue);
        var pImage = Marshal.GetComInterfaceForObject<ID3D12Resource, ID3D12Resource>(resource);
        var external = rd.TextureCreateFromExtension(
            RenderingDevice.TextureType.Type2D,
            RenderingDevice.DataFormat.R16G16B16A16Sfloat,
            RenderingDevice.TextureSamples.Samples1,
            RenderingDevice.TextureUsageBits.SamplingBit
                | RenderingDevice.TextureUsageBits.ColorAttachmentBit
                | RenderingDevice.TextureUsageBits.CanCopyFromBit,
            (ulong)pImage,
            (ulong)target.Size.Width,
            (ulong)target.Size.Height,
            1,
            1
        );
        var display = rd.TextureCreate(
            new RDTextureFormat
            {
                TextureType = RenderingDevice.TextureType.Type2D,
                Format = RenderingDevice.DataFormat.R16G16B16A16Sfloat,
                Samples = RenderingDevice.TextureSamples.Samples1,
                UsageBits =
                    RenderingDevice.TextureUsageBits.SamplingBit
                    | RenderingDevice.TextureUsageBits.ColorAttachmentBit
                    | RenderingDevice.TextureUsageBits.CanCopyToBit
                    | RenderingDevice.TextureUsageBits.CanCopyFromBit,
                Width = (uint)target.Size.Width,
                Height = (uint)target.Size.Height,
                Depth = 1,
                ArrayLayers = 1,
            },
            new RDTextureView()
        );
        rd.TextureCopy(
            external,
            display,
            new Vector3(0, 0, 0),
            new Vector3(0, 0, 0),
            new Vector3(2560, 1440, 1),
            0,
            0,
            0,
            0
        );
        var texture2D = new Texture2Drd { TextureRdRid = display };
        _rid = display;
        Control.Texture = texture2D;
        var image = texture2D.GetImage();
        image?.SaveExr("capture.exr");
        rd.FreeRid(external);
        Marshal.Release(pImage);
    }

    // Called every frame. 'delta' is the elapsed time since the previous frame.
    public override void _Process(double delta) { }

    protected override void Dispose(bool disposing)
    {
        if (_rid.IsValid)
            RenderingServer.GetRenderingDevice().FreeRid(_rid);
        base.Dispose(disposing);
    }
}

```

</details>

After testing, I confirmed that this isn't a blit issue (I was misled by the comments), but rather that brightness adjustment is being applied somewhere else.

The actual issue is that the Windows Reference White level is already being applied when capturing screenshots through WGC/DXGI. Godot then applies it again during the blit, resulting in the adjustment being applied twice.
Similarly, the white point of the EXR images I provided is not EDR 1.0 either.

So I owe you an apology. I misunderstood what you meant earlier, this is not an engine issue.
